WEBVTT

00:00:04.209 --> 00:00:07.037
Welcome to The Lighter Side of the Spectrum, the podcast

00:00:07.037 --> 00:00:10.026
that celebrates the multiple hats we wear and the juggling

00:00:10.138 --> 00:00:13.046
acts we perform in life. Whether you're parenting kids and

00:00:13.046 --> 00:00:17.272
teens on the autism spectrum, navigating the ever-evolving healthcare industry,

00:00:17.545 --> 00:00:20.566
or building a more inclusive and impactful world, this is

00:00:20.598 --> 00:00:25.049
your space to find connection, inspiration, and growth. Together, we'll

00:00:25.097 --> 00:00:29.532
share stories, tackle challenges, and explore how innovation and inclusion

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:32.987
create better outcomes for everyone. Because no matter the spectrum,

00:00:33.292 --> 00:00:36.695
there's always a lighter side. Now here's your host, David

00:00:36.695 --> 00:00:37.090
Koffer.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:48.979
Hello again, everybody, and welcome back to Lighter Side of

00:00:48.979 --> 00:00:50.807
the Spectrum. I'm David Koffer.

00:00:51.626 --> 00:00:55.111
Today we're talking about something I think deserves far more

00:00:55.144 --> 00:00:56.251
attention than it gets. When most

00:00:58.090 --> 00:00:59.825
When most people think about autism,

00:01:00.676 --> 00:01:05.400
they think about communication differences, sensory challenges, repetitive

00:01:05.432 --> 00:01:06.123
behaviors,

00:01:06.862 --> 00:01:08.035
or social interaction.

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:12.100
But what they don't think about usually is trauma.

00:01:13.466 --> 00:01:17.049
Not because trauma is necessarily a part of autism itself,

00:01:17.081 --> 00:01:21.002
but because so many autistic children and adults experience repeated

00:01:21.002 --> 00:01:26.202
events that leave lasting emotional wounds. School struggles, bullying,

00:01:26.635 --> 00:01:28.830
hospitalizations, family disruption,

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:30.913
being misunderstood,

00:01:31.907 --> 00:01:34.486
not being able to communicate, living in a world that

00:01:34.486 --> 00:01:36.666
often wasn't designed with them in mind.

00:01:38.076 --> 00:01:39.678
Those experiences change people,

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.629
and today's guest understands that reality as well as anyone.

00:01:45.607 --> 00:01:49.356
Stephen Lane is a father, autism advocate, and writer whose

00:01:49.533 --> 00:01:54.308
honest Facebook posts have resonated with thousands of families, including

00:01:54.308 --> 00:01:54.548
my own.

00:01:55.622 --> 00:01:59.131
Over the past several months, his family has faced every

00:01:59.163 --> 00:02:00.493
parent's nightmare, including

00:02:01.311 --> 00:02:02.817
psychiatric hospitalization,

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:09.163
repeated elopement, navigating a system that often seems unprepared to

00:02:09.163 --> 00:02:09.419
help.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.864
But through it all, Stephen has continued to share something

00:02:12.864 --> 00:02:14.242
that's incredibly rare—

00:02:15.172 --> 00:02:17.159
not polished success stories,

00:02:17.992 --> 00:02:21.552
But real life, the actual fear, the exhaustion,

00:02:22.546 --> 00:02:26.393
and the hope, and ultimately what healing can look like.

00:02:27.868 --> 00:02:30.145
Stephen, welcome to The Lighter Side of the Spectrum.

00:02:31.139 --> 00:02:31.925
Thank you for having me.

00:02:33.223 --> 00:02:35.917
Yeah, I'm really happy to have you here. I'm glad

00:02:35.933 --> 00:02:38.193
that our paths have crossed. Like I said, one of

00:02:38.193 --> 00:02:39.155
the things that

00:02:40.117 --> 00:02:44.366
struck me is just, uh, your raw honesty, uh, in

00:02:44.366 --> 00:02:47.268
terms of approaching this. You don't always see this. in

00:02:47.268 --> 00:02:48.895
our space in terms of other

00:02:49.732 --> 00:02:53.564
parents who post on Facebook and other

00:02:54.498 --> 00:02:55.029
platforms.

00:02:56.993 --> 00:02:59.363
One of the things you wrote that really grabbed my

00:02:59.395 --> 00:03:02.603
attention was this. You said, I didn't choose advocacy,

00:03:03.925 --> 00:03:05.796
advocacy chose me. Yeah.

00:03:06.925 --> 00:03:10.716
So I think every parent probably listening to this right

00:03:10.765 --> 00:03:13.459
now understands exactly what you meant, but I'd like you

00:03:13.684 --> 00:03:15.366
to talk about what it means to you and tell

00:03:15.416 --> 00:03:15.618
us

00:03:16.532 --> 00:03:17.221
about your journey?

00:03:18.198 --> 00:03:20.458
Well, okay. Um, so I will, um,

00:03:21.179 --> 00:03:22.781
kind of go back in time a little bit here.

00:03:22.862 --> 00:03:25.890
So yeah, please, we'll go. Yeah, so we're gonna go

00:03:25.906 --> 00:03:28.486
back to the start of when I went into the

00:03:28.775 --> 00:03:31.563
autism world, as you could say. And that was in

00:03:31.595 --> 00:03:32.621
2011.

00:03:33.342 --> 00:03:37.284
Um, I was living in Oxford, Michigan. And for those

00:03:37.284 --> 00:03:39.143
of your listeners who don't know where Oxford is, that's

00:03:39.143 --> 00:03:43.488
about 40 miles north of Detroit. Okay. It's a semi-rural

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:44.177
area.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:46.552
So anyways,

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:48.766
we're noticing, my

00:03:50.113 --> 00:03:53.787
wife at the time, we were both noticing, I'm divorced

00:03:53.787 --> 00:03:56.290
right now and I'll get into that later. Yeah, but

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:58.135
we definitely want to get into that. I know that's

00:03:58.135 --> 00:03:59.771
a big part of your story too. It is part

00:03:59.771 --> 00:04:01.247
of my story too, but I'll get into that later.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.402
So anyways, in 2011, a lot of people were going

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:08.338
to me and my wife at the time, They were

00:04:08.338 --> 00:04:11.591
noticing certain things with Matthew, like he wasn't hitting certain

00:04:11.591 --> 00:04:15.821
milestones. He wasn't walking at the appropriate age. He wasn't

00:04:15.837 --> 00:04:16.846
communicating at all.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:19.987
He was just communicating with hums,

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:21.781
as I would like to say.

00:04:22.566 --> 00:04:25.178
And he was about 3 and a half to 4

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:26.059
years old at the time.

00:04:26.813 --> 00:04:27.069
Okay.

00:04:28.094 --> 00:04:31.123
And so, you know, everyone thinks, you know, there's something

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:34.664
going on with him. Is it hearing impairment? Is it

00:04:34.744 --> 00:04:37.409
something else? So one of the first things I do,

00:04:37.473 --> 00:04:38.614
I get his hearing tested.

00:04:39.497 --> 00:04:42.695
Came back. Yep, he came back normal. So it wasn't

00:04:42.695 --> 00:04:42.904
that.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:44.607
So then

00:04:45.346 --> 00:04:46.889
I enrolled him into preschool

00:04:47.837 --> 00:04:50.906
through our school district. And they're thinking autism.

00:04:52.176 --> 00:04:53.879
And I'm disagreeing with them.

00:04:54.586 --> 00:04:56.900
I was gonna say, were you thinking autism at all?

00:04:56.948 --> 00:04:59.792
No, I wasn't. No, I wasn't. No, I wasn't.

00:05:00.692 --> 00:05:04.027
And so I'm disagreeing with them. And so I'm like,

00:05:04.091 --> 00:05:06.671
well, I'm gonna go find some doctors, and I'm gonna

00:05:06.671 --> 00:05:08.670
see if I can get him, you know, see what

00:05:08.686 --> 00:05:09.185
they say.

00:05:11.422 --> 00:05:14.453
Well, I got 2 opinions, 2 different opinions. And it

00:05:14.453 --> 00:05:15.754
was the same thing, autism.

00:05:17.735 --> 00:05:18.398
And so

00:05:19.223 --> 00:05:20.856
I'm like, well, this is a done deal.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.818
And I still wasn't able to come to terms with

00:05:24.851 --> 00:05:25.028
it.

00:05:25.756 --> 00:05:26.969
And that's fine. Now,

00:05:27.746 --> 00:05:29.607
I got a younger son by the name of Aiden.

00:05:30.409 --> 00:05:31.965
At the time, he was 2 and a half.

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:35.029
Okay. And I rolled him into preschool.

00:05:36.489 --> 00:05:39.345
And I remember taking him to preschool first day.

00:05:40.419 --> 00:05:40.949
And he's

00:05:41.703 --> 00:05:44.526
not communicating with any of the peers there. He's just

00:05:44.526 --> 00:05:49.612
stacking blocks upon blocks upon blocks, perfect alignment,

00:05:50.366 --> 00:05:51.986
and humming to the blocks.

00:05:53.012 --> 00:05:54.633
Now, at this point, I already did a little bit

00:05:54.633 --> 00:05:57.055
of homework and knew what the signs are for autism

00:05:57.055 --> 00:06:00.026
at this point. Sure. Right. And I'm like, oh, here

00:06:00.026 --> 00:06:03.738
we go again. So I guess he's gotta get diagnosed.

00:06:03.786 --> 00:06:06.422
And so I got him diagnosed, you know, at the

00:06:06.422 --> 00:06:08.302
same time, went through the same process, went to a

00:06:08.302 --> 00:06:10.776
natural doctor, got him diagnosed. So

00:06:11.885 --> 00:06:15.436
he was diagnosed. So, and at the time, once Aiden's

00:06:15.436 --> 00:06:18.698
diagnosis came through, I started looking at ABA services.

00:06:19.694 --> 00:06:22.748
Well, at the time in Michigan, ABA wasn't covered under

00:06:22.748 --> 00:06:23.487
insurance.

00:06:24.418 --> 00:06:27.373
So, and it was— there wasn't all these ABA centers

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:28.769
around like there is today.

00:06:29.813 --> 00:06:33.586
So I inquired about it. And they said, well, it's

00:06:33.586 --> 00:06:37.312
gonna cost you about $30,000 a year out of pocket.

00:06:37.841 --> 00:06:37.938
Wow.

00:06:38.869 --> 00:06:41.342
So that's crazy. And like I said, at the time,

00:06:41.856 --> 00:06:45.340
insurances in Michigan didn't cover it. So we're like, well,

00:06:45.629 --> 00:06:48.294
this is beyond my own— my means, you know, I

00:06:48.294 --> 00:06:51.421
don't have I don't have $30 grand. $30 grand sitting

00:06:51.421 --> 00:06:53.680
around to— Sit around just to fork over for 1

00:06:53.712 --> 00:06:55.138
year of ABA services.

00:06:56.597 --> 00:06:58.423
So I took what I could get as far as

00:06:58.536 --> 00:07:01.548
therapies, what I could find at the time.

00:07:02.285 --> 00:07:04.673
And so we did that for quite a few years.

00:07:05.891 --> 00:07:09.320
And then probably fast forward to 2019,

00:07:10.393 --> 00:07:12.621
my wife and I were having a lot of serious

00:07:12.621 --> 00:07:16.723
issues at that point and we got divorced. Eventually got

00:07:16.723 --> 00:07:18.694
divorced. I'll get into that. But we separated

00:07:19.431 --> 00:07:20.809
in September

00:07:21.594 --> 00:07:24.783
of 2019. The boys switched schools

00:07:26.081 --> 00:07:30.824
from Oxford to where they're presently at right now. Okay.

00:07:30.904 --> 00:07:34.045
And, and so I'm not like near Oxford or anything.

00:07:34.093 --> 00:07:37.090
I'm more closer. I'm in like suburban Detroit right now.

00:07:38.115 --> 00:07:38.436
So

00:07:39.381 --> 00:07:42.938
that was a big transition for the boys. And they

00:07:43.067 --> 00:07:46.271
were really traumatized from it because again, they're autistic and

00:07:46.271 --> 00:07:48.658
they can only understand certain concepts.

00:07:49.716 --> 00:07:53.529
Many children don't understand the concept of divorce, but when

00:07:53.577 --> 00:07:57.342
you put that on an autistic child, Mm-hmm. the trauma's

00:07:57.342 --> 00:07:59.650
gonna be there. And that kind of goes towards, like

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:01.540
I was saying in a lot of my posts, where

00:08:01.973 --> 00:08:04.681
autism care and trauma do go hand in hand.

00:08:05.498 --> 00:08:08.013
You know, they were wondering why we weren't together anymore.

00:08:08.237 --> 00:08:11.390
They were wondering why we had 2 separate homes. which

00:08:11.390 --> 00:08:13.450
it was like at the time, we were in 2

00:08:13.450 --> 00:08:15.870
separate homes, you know, they were living in one home

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:18.630
part of the time, they were living at my, at

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:22.490
their mother's place. So I just want, I just want

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:25.110
a quick question here, Steve. And that is, so it

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:28.550
sounds like both boys, again, they were diagnosed, but sounds

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:31.670
like they were able to gain some functionality to communicate,

00:08:31.670 --> 00:08:34.410
like, so they've been, they've been able to speak to

00:08:34.410 --> 00:08:36.770
a certain degree. Would you say they have limited vocabulary

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:40.210
or, you know, communication skills? Or, you know, because it

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:41.890
sounds like they didn't, that they were You said they

00:08:41.906 --> 00:08:44.905
were confused about the living situation, which is understandable. Yeah,

00:08:44.921 --> 00:08:47.215
I'm sorry, fast forward to that. But yeah, no, it's

00:08:47.215 --> 00:08:48.017
okay. Yeah.

00:08:48.803 --> 00:08:52.204
But back to when they were in elementary school, I

00:08:52.268 --> 00:08:54.449
want to say by the time they hit kindergarten, they

00:08:54.449 --> 00:08:58.026
were starting to get somewhat verbal. Now, my youngest son,

00:08:58.123 --> 00:09:00.833
Aiden, was more verbal at a younger age than Matthew

00:09:00.833 --> 00:09:05.469
was. Matthew didn't start becoming verbal till about 5 years

00:09:05.565 --> 00:09:05.646
old.

00:09:06.528 --> 00:09:09.078
So that's around the time he started saying his first

00:09:09.078 --> 00:09:12.849
words. Um, eventually we were able to get into very

00:09:13.122 --> 00:09:17.838
basic conversations with both of them. Okay. Okay. Yeah. So,

00:09:17.854 --> 00:09:21.704
okay. So please continue. So, so that's the background. So,

00:09:21.849 --> 00:09:25.602
you know, again, in 2019, it's gotta be so hard.

00:09:25.715 --> 00:09:26.469
I mean, again, your

00:09:27.383 --> 00:09:29.789
relationships are challenging. I mean, that's a, that's a whole

00:09:29.789 --> 00:09:32.372
nother conversation I can have with so many people talking

00:09:32.372 --> 00:09:32.661
about

00:09:34.041 --> 00:09:38.519
balancing and managing Marriages, relationships when you're dealing with one,

00:09:38.535 --> 00:09:40.703
let alone 2 autistic kids. So you guys, you know,

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:43.627
going through these challenges and you're trying to manage the

00:09:43.675 --> 00:09:44.189
trauma

00:09:45.056 --> 00:09:46.823
of changing school districts.

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:50.324
Yeah. Yeah. So we went through that. So that's in

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:51.850
September 2019.

00:09:52.588 --> 00:09:56.106
And my youngest son, Aiden, was having meltdowns at school

00:09:56.941 --> 00:09:59.639
just because he was unable to cope with the changes

00:09:59.752 --> 00:10:00.410
at the time.

00:10:01.502 --> 00:10:03.429
He wanted to go back to his old school, didn't

00:10:03.446 --> 00:10:08.040
want to go to where he was presently going. Matthew

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:10.680
was also still having some issues. He wasn't having any

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:15.000
meltdowns, but he was acting very hyper in the classroom

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:16.670
and he wasn't even focusing.

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:23.300
So let's fast forward to 3 months later. Yeah, probably

00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:25.140
maybe not 3 months, maybe more like 4 to 5

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:29.840
months later, COVID happens. Right, right, right. Yeah. Because you're

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:32.940
going— yeah, we're going from 2019, obviously, into early 2020.

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:37.233
Yeah, COVID hits. Good times. COVID hits. So we're all

00:10:37.442 --> 00:10:40.235
in lockdown. You know, we can't go anywhere, kids can't

00:10:40.235 --> 00:10:42.112
go to school, we're going to— they're going to Zoom,

00:10:42.353 --> 00:10:45.515
they're going to school over Zoom. Yeah. And, you know,

00:10:45.531 --> 00:10:47.152
they're wondering what's going on here.

00:10:47.939 --> 00:10:51.421
And so I ended up getting in touch with a

00:10:51.486 --> 00:10:54.038
therapist for both my boys, because all the stuff they've

00:10:54.038 --> 00:10:57.585
been through at that point. Well, again, during the lockdown

00:10:57.585 --> 00:10:58.162
sessions,

00:10:58.949 --> 00:10:59.671
they're over Zoom.

00:11:00.618 --> 00:11:04.163
Yeah. You try to do therapy with an autistic child

00:11:04.228 --> 00:11:06.827
over Zoom. For some, it might work. I don't think

00:11:06.827 --> 00:11:09.121
it really worked for my boys, really, to be honest

00:11:09.121 --> 00:11:11.865
with you. Yeah, it's hard. It's a challenge for sure.

00:11:11.929 --> 00:11:13.036
It is. It was.

00:11:14.335 --> 00:11:16.581
And so eventually, you know,

00:11:17.303 --> 00:11:18.057
lockdowns,

00:11:19.068 --> 00:11:22.903
you know, we eased back into the public school setting

00:11:24.122 --> 00:11:27.803
once things are opening up. My youngest son, my— both

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:30.251
my sons at this point were going to middle school.

00:11:31.442 --> 00:11:32.006
And so

00:11:33.777 --> 00:11:36.716
I actually, which was really fortunate because by this point

00:11:36.862 --> 00:11:39.257
COVID happened, they had a break from everything, from being

00:11:39.273 --> 00:11:40.714
on lockdown, which

00:11:41.459 --> 00:11:43.142
is ironic as it sounds. I was actually kind of

00:11:43.174 --> 00:11:44.144
grateful for that,

00:11:44.854 --> 00:11:47.823
you know, everything they've been through. Right, right. Kind of

00:11:47.839 --> 00:11:51.421
paused everything in a way. Exactly. Yes, it did. Yeah.

00:11:51.712 --> 00:11:53.357
And so once they were able to go back to

00:11:54.054 --> 00:11:56.943
you know, an actual school setting, they started thriving, both

00:11:56.959 --> 00:11:58.988
of them. And they were both in middle school at

00:11:58.988 --> 00:11:59.410
that point.

00:12:01.982 --> 00:12:05.703
So let's fast forward a little bit. So now we're

00:12:05.703 --> 00:12:08.965
back in the school setting. So 2021 hits.

00:12:10.158 --> 00:12:13.402
And so at that point, and which I'm going to

00:12:13.402 --> 00:12:14.531
get in a little bit of detail, but I can't

00:12:14.531 --> 00:12:17.033
go fully detailed because it's kind of personal, a little

00:12:17.033 --> 00:12:18.860
too personal. Yeah, yeah, I understand.

00:12:19.756 --> 00:12:23.426
I ended up taking over for both my boys

00:12:24.179 --> 00:12:27.384
to where they were here with me full-time, 24/7.

00:12:28.169 --> 00:12:32.271
And this happened in October of 2021. Again, this was

00:12:32.271 --> 00:12:35.011
another transition. They want to know why they were

00:12:36.357 --> 00:12:38.841
no longer living with their mother. They wanted to know

00:12:39.146 --> 00:12:41.165
what happened. Oh yeah. They lost all their stuff, all

00:12:41.165 --> 00:12:43.328
their belongings. So I had to replace everything that they

00:12:43.472 --> 00:12:47.106
owned that they had at her house. Oh, wow. Yeah.

00:12:47.235 --> 00:12:50.085
So, and this, this was very traumatic for them. And,

00:12:50.133 --> 00:12:50.664
you know, they were—

00:12:51.518 --> 00:12:54.303
there was some regression when they were in school with

00:12:54.335 --> 00:12:55.108
both boys.

00:12:56.557 --> 00:12:59.974
But I want to say after probably about 3 to

00:12:59.974 --> 00:13:03.537
6 months, we did get through that. And, you know,

00:13:03.554 --> 00:13:05.449
they were able to move on successfully.

00:13:08.459 --> 00:13:10.189
And so every day you're— I mean, you're— this is

00:13:10.189 --> 00:13:12.621
something that you still had to manage. I mean, it's

00:13:12.621 --> 00:13:13.339
kind of funny, kind of

00:13:14.167 --> 00:13:16.123
Kind of gloss over it like, oh, 3 to 6

00:13:16.123 --> 00:13:18.287
months, you know, they kind of got over it. But

00:13:18.287 --> 00:13:19.441
in the meantime, you're still

00:13:20.259 --> 00:13:22.792
trying to manage their lives and doing this on your

00:13:22.840 --> 00:13:25.180
own. I mean, you're essentially a single dad, I assume,

00:13:25.309 --> 00:13:26.495
working full-time and,

00:13:27.633 --> 00:13:28.403
and dealing with them.

00:13:29.252 --> 00:13:30.807
I do, but I also get a lot of help

00:13:30.807 --> 00:13:33.100
from my mother. Okay. And she helps me out with

00:13:33.100 --> 00:13:35.889
the boys. So when I'm at work, she's watching them.

